        "original_id": "09403362-cef8-5c10-bef5-c4d88df3cf1b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Rousillon Rupes, Titania\n- People: William Shakespeare, Bertram, count of Rousillon\n- Works: All's Well That Ends Well, Measure for Measure, Troilus and Cressida, First Folio\n- Organizations: Voyager 2 spacecraft\n- Concepts: Shakespearean problem plays\n\nFacts:\n- William Shakespeare is the author of All's Well That Ends Well.\n- All's Well That Ends Well is believed to have been written between 1604 and 1605.\n- All's Well That Ends Well was originally published in the First Folio in 1623.\n- In Shakespeare studies, the Shakespearean problem plays are All's Well That Ends Well, Measure for Measure, and Troilus and Cressida, written between the late 1590s and the first years of the seventeenth century.\n- Voyager 2 first imaged Rousillon Rupes in January 1986.\n- Rousillon Rupes is a scarp on the surface of Titania.\n- The name Rousillon Rupes comes from Bertram, count of Rousillon, an Elizabethan English misspelling of Roussillon, in All's Well That Ends Well.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
